DEF-FORMS AS MACROS + LEXICAL BLOCKS (pjb, 2026-07)

SYMPTOM (pjb report):
- DEFUN / IN-PACKAGE / DEFVAR ... are open-coded special forms in eval.c, but
  (special-operator-p 'defun) => NIL and they have NO macro definition
  ((macroexpand '(in-package ...)) / (macroexpand '(defun ...)) return unchanged,
  (symbol-function 'defun) is unbound).  So they are neither special operators nor
  macros — an inconsistent limbo.
- Downstream: (function-lambda-expression 'fact) returned (LAMBDA (X) <body>)
  WITHOUT the implicit (BLOCK FACT ...) that a real DEFUN establishes, because the
  block was created only at run time (eval_body_in_block, keyed on lisp_name) and
  never stored in the function body.  return-from worked but FLE/macroexpand/fasl
  did not reflect the block, and a compiler could not turn the (lexical) block into
  branches.

DECISION (pjb-confirmed): they are ANSI MACROS, not special operators
(special-operator-p stays NIL — making it T would be non-conforming).  Give them
proper macro definitions; make the implicit block LEXICAL (part of the stored
body); fully expand them in the fasl, aided by an implementation operator
BOCL:DEFINE-FUNCTION.  The block special operator interprets the lexical block, so
a named function has two frames — the call frame (eval_body_in_block) and the
block frame (the lexical BLOCK) — while an anonymous LAMBDA has only the call
frame.

PHASE 1 (v1.5.3) — DONE: LEXICAL BLOCKS.
- New eval.c helper make_block_body(name, body): wraps the body in a lexical
  (BLOCK name form...), keeping leading (declare ...) forms AND a documentation
  string OUTSIDE the block (BLOCK evaluates its forms, so a declare left inside
  would be evaluated as a call).  skip_declares_and_doc() skips an interleaved
  docstring (only when more forms follow — a lone trailing string is a return
  value).  Every cons auto-roots in the live pool, so no manual rooting.
- DEFUN (symbol and (setf foo)), FLET, LABELS, and DEFMACRO now store
  block-wrapped bodies.  FUNCTION-LAMBDA-EXPRESSION shows the block; return-from
  the function name resolves via the lexical block.  eval_body_in_block (the call
  frame) is unchanged, so the debugger :CALL backtrace is intact and the run-time
  block is now a harmless redundant safety net (a future compiler uses the lexical
  one).  Full unit suite (42) green; new regression tests in test_slice25.

PHASE 2 (TODO) — DEF-FORMS AS MACROS + FASL EXPANSION:
- BOCL:DEFINE-FUNCTION primitive (name-preserving install: lisp_name = name).
- DEFUN macro with proper body parsing (docstring -> DOCUMENTATION; declarations
  kept at the lambda level / wrapped in LOCALLY / treated / ignored as
  appropriate), expanding to
  (bocl:define-function 'name (lambda ll decl* (block name form*))) + doc.
- Macro definitions for IN-PACKAGE / DEFVAR / DEFPARAMETER / DEFCONSTANT so
  MACROEXPAND expands them; MACROEXPAND-ALL / the fasl then expand them (fully
  macro-free fasl).  eval keeps a fast path (C special form checked first, or the
  C DEFUN delegates to the macro post-bootstrap) — kept consistent with the macro.

PHASE 2a (v1.5.4) — DONE: DEFUN IS A MACRO.
- New C primitive BOCL:DEFINE-FUNCTION (bocl.c): the single, name-preserving
  function-definition mechanism the DEFUN macro expands to.  It installs a
  function under a symbol with lisp_name = that symbol (rebuilding an anonymous
  (LAMBDA ...) closure so FLE / backtraces report the real name), keeping a
  closure's captured environment; a built-in is stored unchanged.  Registered and
  exported in the BOCL package.  (The natural home for future compile-time
  bookkeeping.)
- DEFUN macro (prelude, with %SPLIT-BODY-HEAD mirroring the C skip_declares_and_doc):
  (defun name ll . body) -> (bocl:define-function 'name
     #'(lambda ll <decls+doc> (block name . forms))), and (defun (setf foo) ...) ->
  (%register-setf-function 'foo #'(lambda ll ... (block foo . forms))) — the kernel
  names functions with symbols only, so (setf foo) is handled at the Lisp level.
  eval still uses the C DEFUN (special-form dispatch precedes macro dispatch), and
  the macro produces the SAME shape, so eval and MACROEXPAND agree (verified: FLE
  identical, name preserved, block intact).  MACROEXPAND-ALL / COMPILE-FILE now
  fully expand DEFUN (the fasl carries BOCL:DEFINE-FUNCTION forms, no DEFUN), and
  round-trips (return-from included).  special-operator-p 'defun stays NIL.  Full
  unit suite (42) green; new test_slice25 "defun macro".

PHASE 2b (TODO): macro-functions for IN-PACKAGE (needs a %IN-PACKAGE primitive
exposing bocl_in_package, expanded inside an EVAL-WHEN so compile-file's
compile-time package tracking still fires) and DEFVAR / DEFPARAMETER / DEFCONSTANT
(expand to proclaim-special + conditional set, matching the C special forms).
Docstring -> DOCUMENTATION extraction is still deferred (kept in the lambda body,
as the C DEFUN does — no drift).

PHASE 2b (v1.5.5 doc-extraction, v1.5.6 macros) — DONE.  ISSUE CLOSED.
- v1.5.5: DEFUN extracts the docstring into (documentation name 'function).
  make_block_body gained a DOC_OUT (extract for DEFUN, keep in body for
  FLET/LABELS/DEFMACRO); bocl_set_function_documentation (eval.c, shared via
  eval.h) records it through the Lisp %SET-FUNCTION-DOCUMENTATION helper; both the
  interpreter's DEFUN and BOCL:DEFINE-FUNCTION (now (name documentation function))
  call it, so eval and fasl agree.  A lone trailing string stays a return value.
- v1.5.6: IN-PACKAGE / DEFVAR / DEFPARAMETER / DEFCONSTANT are now macros too.
  New kernel seams %IN-PACKAGE / %DEFINE-VARIABLE / %DEFINE-VARIABLE-UNBOUND /
  %DEFINE-PARAMETER / %DEFINE-CONSTANT forward to the SAME primitives
  (bocl_in_package, variable.c define_*) the eval.c special forms use, so a
  fasl-loaded expansion behaves identically to eval.  IN-PACKAGE expands inside an
  EVAL-WHEN (:compile-toplevel :load-toplevel :execute) so compile-file's
  compile-time package tracking still fires; DEFVAR guards init-form evaluation to
  the unbound case.  eval keeps the C special forms (dispatch precedes macros);
  special-operator-p stays NIL for all of them.
Net: a compiled file is now FULLY macro-free — DEFUN / DEFVAR / DEFPARAMETER /
DEFCONSTANT / IN-PACKAGE / DEFPACKAGE all expand to primitives — and round-trips
(multi-package + return-from verified).  Full unit suite (42) green throughout;
new test_slice25 "def-form macros".
